### What is @ElementCollection?

@ElementCollection maps a collection of basic types or @Embeddable value objects into a separate collection table that is fully owned by the parent entity. The elements have no @Id, no independent lifecycle, and cannot be queried on their own. They are loaded and saved entirely as part of the parent.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "balance", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // Collection of BASIC values
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_phones", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @Column(name = "phone")
    private List<String> phoneNumbers = new ArrayList<>();

    // Collection of EMBEDDABLE value objects
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_nominees", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @OrderColumn(name = "position")     // avoids delete-all-reinsert
    private List<Nominee> nominees = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Embeddable;

@Embeddable
public class Nominee {
    private String name;
    private String relationship;
    private int sharePercent;
    // NO @Id -- it is a value, not an entity

    public Nominee() {}

    public Nominee(String name, String relationship, int sharePercent) {
        this.name = name;
        this.relationship = relationship;
        this.sharePercent = sharePercent;
    }

    // getters
}
```

```text
Generated tables:
- account_phones: account_id (FK), phone (VARCHAR)
- account_nominees: account_id (FK), position (INT), name, relationship, share_percent
- Neither table has its own primary key (unless @OrderColumn provides one)
```

### What is @OneToMany?

@OneToMany maps a relationship to independent entities. Each child has its own @Id, its own table, and its own lifecycle. You can query the child directly, reference it from other entities, and manage it independently.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "trade_id")
    private String tradeId;               // has its own identity

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private Account account;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
Trade is an ENTITY because:
- It has its own @Id (tradeId)
- It has its own table (trades)
- Auditors query trades directly
- It has an independent lifecycle
```

### How do @ElementCollection and @OneToMany compare?

| Aspect | @ElementCollection | @OneToMany |
|---|---|---|
| Child type | Basic type or @Embeddable | Entity with @Id |
| Own table | Collection table (no PK) | Entity table (has PK) |
| Identity | No @Id, no independent existence | Has @Id, queryable directly |
| Lifecycle | Owned entirely by parent | Independent lifecycle |
| Cascade | Automatic (always cascaded) | Configurable (CascadeType) |
| Sharing | Cannot be shared between parents | Can be shared / referenced |
| Update behaviour | Delete-all-reinsert (without @OrderColumn) | Individual row updates |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Nominee;
import com.bank.model.Trade;

public class ComparisonDemo {
    public void demonstrate(Account account) {
        // @ElementCollection: nominees owned by account, no independent query
        account.getNominees().add(new Nominee("Priya", "spouse", 50));
        // Saving the account saves the nominee automatically

        // @OneToMany: trades are independent entities
        Trade trade = new Trade("T001", account, new java.math.BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        // Trade can be queried: tradeRepository.findById("T001")
        // Trade can exist independently of Account in other contexts
    }
}
```

### What is the delete-all-reinsert problem?

If the collection table has no primary key and no @OrderColumn, Hibernate cannot identify individual rows. On ANY modification, Hibernate performs a DELETE of all rows for that parent followed by a full REINSERT. For a nominee list of three, this is harmless. For a large value collection, it is a performance trap.

```java
// WRONG: no @OrderColumn -> delete-all-reinsert on every change
@ElementCollection
@CollectionTable(name = "account_phones", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
@Column(name = "phone")
private List<String> phoneNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
// Adding one phone number:
// DELETE FROM account_phones WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone) VALUES ('SAV2001', '+91-11111')
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone) VALUES ('SAV2001', '+91-22222')
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone) VALUES ('SAV2001', '+91-33333')  <- the new one
```

```java
// CORRECT: @OrderColumn allows individual row updates
@ElementCollection
@CollectionTable(name = "account_phones", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
@Column(name = "phone")
@OrderColumn(name = "position")
private List<String> phoneNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
// Adding one phone number:
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone, position) VALUES ('SAV2001', '+91-33333', 2)
// Only ONE INSERT, no delete-reinsert!
```

### When should you use @ElementCollection vs @OneToMany?

Ask: does this child have an identity, and would anyone ever load or query it on its own? If yes, it is an entity and use @OneToMany. If it is just descriptive data attached to one parent and never shared, it is a value object and use @ElementCollection.

```java
package com.bank.model;

// Value object: no identity, owned by parent
@Embeddable
public class Nominee {
    private String name;
    private String relationship;
    private int sharePercent;
    // Nobody queries "all nominees" as first-class records
}

// Entity: has identity, queried independently
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    private String tradeId;
    // Auditors query trades directly, settlement system references them
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using @ElementCollection for a child that needs to be shared between parents. Value objects cannot be shared. The second mistake is forgetting @OrderColumn on a large element collection.

```java
// WRONG: trying to share a value object between two parents
public class WrongExample {
    public void share() {
        Nominee priya = new Nominee("Priya", "spouse", 100);
        account1.getNominees().add(priya);
        account2.getNominees().add(priya);  // WRONG: Priya is duplicated in both tables
        // Each parent owns its own COPY. If you update one, the other is unaffected.
        // If you need sharing, Nominee must be an @Entity with @ManyToMany
    }
}
```

### What about the default fetch type?

@ElementCollection defaults to LAZY, which is usually what you want. The collection is loaded only when you access it. If you need it eagerly, you can set fetch = FetchType.EAGER, but this is rarely correct for collections.

```java
// Default: LAZY (correct for most cases)
@ElementCollection                     // defaults to FetchType.LAZY
@CollectionTable(name = "account_nominees")
private List<Nominee> nominees;

// When you need them: use JOIN FETCH in a query
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a LEFT JOIN FETCH a.nominees WHERE a.accountId = :id")
Account findWithNominees(String id);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows about the delete-all-reinsert trap and always adds @OrderColumn to list-based element collections. They know that value objects cannot be shared between parents. They use @ElementCollection for small, parent-owned collections (phone numbers, tags, nominees) and @OneToMany for anything with its own identity.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Account {

    @Id
    private String accountId;

    // Senior pattern: @OrderColumn + small owned collection
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_nominees", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @OrderColumn(name = "position")
    private List<Nominee> nominees = new ArrayList<>();

    // Entity relationship for independent children
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
}
```
